research.TCA_ANCIENTS.title=Disturbing Thoughts
research.TCA_ANCIENTS.text.stage.1=Sometimes I feel as if there are things that elude even my tenacious mind. I am increasingly haunted by a thought that seems to transcend any madness.<BR>Is it possible that the ancient revelations I've relied on until now are nothing more than a smokescreen? An illusion, a clever trick meant to distract me from something far more important?<BR>This knowledge is certainly impressive. It intrigues and shocks. However, the price one must pay for it often proves disproportionately high. It's as if the Void itself is playing a shell game with me.<BR>In search of answers, I have explored the darkest corners of the world. I have peered into its depths, traveled through the Nether, the End, the Outlands... and found nothing. Nothing that I haven't encountered before.<BR>Perhaps I should use the augmented Thaumometer to advance further?
research.TCA_ANCIENTS.text.stage.2=What is this? It seems this augment has far more properties than I initially imagined. The Thaumometer revealed inscriptions on the keys from the labyrinth that I couldn't decipher without it. And the strangest thing is their language. I... understand it.
But what worries me most is *what* is written. There's no coherent text here, only fragments, and even in those, some details seem irretrievably lost. But the things I can read shed light on this world's past.<BR>And if these artifacts, scratched as if in great haste, are true, everything is far more terrifying than I could have imagined.<BR>I should move deeper into the ancient structures. I can't keep the keys, as they are needed to open the doors – but I can take imprints from them using a blank seal. To do this, I simply combine it with the revealed key in the crafting grid and then try not to lose it.<BR>When I collect enough, I might finally be able to put the puzzle together. And I already know who will help me with it.
research.TCA_ANCIENTS.text.stage.3=When the infusion was complete, I felt the air around me tremble and my hands shake. Had I really succeeded? Had I truly accomplished something great, and not fallen into yet another of the same delusions I'd repeatedly plucked from the void?<BR>I immediately grabbed the red tablet of hard wax and began to read its contents, not even reaching the research table. The text was written in several layers, words were often repeated, some were crossed out, and others were missing again, but my mind was crystal clear. I understood everything that was here...<BR>...<BR>No, this can't be...<BR>...<BR>Catastrophic mistake! Insane misinterpretation!<BR>Now I understand. The text on the tablet blinds me, passing through my eyes, burning the very connections in my brain, but I understand.<BR>Those beings I called Ancients never had anything to do with them. These mindless phantoms, rotting carcasses, delirious visions... Of course! How foolish was I to think so! Could they have collected libraries, studied and built altars, raised spires to the heavens, and, finally, resisted the void?<BR>Resisted, indeed. I doubted it before, but now I have irrefutable proof that the Outlands were destroyed by war. Although... no. The tablet's account speaks more of *total annihilation*. Constant ruptures of the void created this imitation of life, Eldritch, in the places where they touched matter. Waves of black-violet swarms engulfed the world until it was completely dismembered and digested.<BR>The Ancients... the data I've collected is scant on their inventions. However, I can say with certainty that the materials from which the keys are made are absolutely unique and have never been encountered before.<BR>And I know how to reproduce them.

